steps.message:
The bug was that `Progress.stop()` would print a blank line on non-interactive consoles even when the progress display was disabled (`disabled=True`). This happened because the `stop` method lacked a check for `self.disable`, whereas the `start` method had one.

I updated `rich/progress.py` to wrap the contents of the `stop` method in an `if not self.disable:` block, ensuring symmetry with the `start` method and preventing the extraneous blank line.

```python
    def stop(self) -> None:
        """Stop the progress display."""
        if not self.disable:
            self.live.stop()
            if not self.console.is_interactive and not self.console.is_jupyter:
                self.console.print()
```
